  "preamble": "The shift from a voiced velar stop to the voiced bilabial stop [b] arises from the labialized velar [gʷ] (a velar stop with lip rounding): the velar closure is lost and the lip gesture becomes a full bilabial closure. Proto-Indo-European *gʷ became b in Greek (Mycenaean still writes gʷ, as in gʷōns 'cows') and in Proto-Celtic, e.g. PIE *gʷṓws 'cow' > Greek βοῦς, PIE *gʷih₃wós 'alive' > Proto-Celtic *biwos (Old Irish béo).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Labialization, Place Assimilation",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Ancient Greek (from Proto-Greek)",
      "languageFamily": "Hellenic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Greek *gʷous 'cow, ox' (< PIE *gʷṓws; Mycenaean gʷōns)",
          "to": "βοῦς boûs"
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Greek *gʷarus 'heavy' (< PIE *gʷréh₂us)",
          "to": "βαρύς barús",
          "note": "Compare Latin gravis and Sanskrit gurú, which keep a velar."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
